## If That Produces a Value

In many languages, `if` is purely a statement — it controls flow but doesn't produce a value. In XIOM, `if` can also be an **expression** that returns a value!

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let score = 85;
  let result = if score >= 60 { "Pass" } else { "Fail" };
  io.println(result);
  return 0;
}
```

### How It Works

When `if` is used as an expression, BOTH branches must produce a value of the same type:

```xiom
// Both branches return Int — OK!
let fee = if is_member { 10 } else { 25 };

// Both branches return Str — OK!
let status = if active { "online" } else { "offline" };

// Different types — ERROR!
// let x = if cond { 42 } else { "hello" };  // Int vs Str!
```

### Expressing Conditions Concisely

```xiom
fn get_ticket_price(age: Int) -> Int {
  return if age < 12 { 5 } elif age >= 65 { 7 } else { 10 };
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let price = get_ticket_price(10);  // 5
  return price;
}
```

### Statement vs Expression

```xiom
// Statement form — doesn't produce a value
if is_weekend {
  io.println("Sleep in!");
} else {
  io.println("Wake up!");
}

// Expression form — produces a value
let message = if is_weekend { "Sleep in!" } else { "Wake up!" };
io.println(message);
```

### No Semicolons Inside Expression Branches

```xiom
// Expression form — last value in each branch is returned
let status = if score >= 60 {
  "Pass"  // NO semicolon! This is the returned value
} else {
  "Fail"  // NO semicolon!
};
```

This connects to **tail expressions** (covered later): the last line of a block without a semicolon becomes the block's value.

